 ## Background
-The concept of multi-view voxelization originated in the context of real-time ray-traced global illumination, where efficient voxelization of triangle meshes was required for radiance caching and cone tracing. The specific "3-pass voxelization" technique was documented in *Real-Time Ray Traced Voxel Global Illumination* (2026), which highlighted the issue of cracks in single-axis voxelization and proposed the 3-axis solution as a simple, effective fix [S1, unofficial]. The sheet-buffer approach was developed to address hardware limitations regarding direct 3D texture writes on consumer GPUs, allowing for efficient voxelization on standard graphics pipelines [S2, unofficial].
+The concept of multi-view voxelization originated as a solution to the "crack" problem in single-axis rasterization-based voxelization, where triangles parallel to the projection direction result in missing voxels or topological holes. The "3-pass voxelization" technique, described in *Real-Time Ray Traced Voxel Global Illumination*, formalized the use of three orthogonal passes to ensure complete coverage [S1, unofficial]. This approach became a standard baseline for GPU-based voxelization pipelines, particularly for real-time global illumination and radiosity, where topological correctness is critical for accurate light transport simulation. Variations such as the worksheet synthesis method were developed to address hardware limitations regarding direct 3D texture rendering and to optimize memory bandwidth [S2, unofficial].
 
